{"name":"Curaçao Blue","hex":"#008894","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"curaçao","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A liqueur made from eau de vie, sugar, and dried peel of sweet and sour oranges, naturally colorless but typically artificially colored blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cura%C3%A7ao"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Curaçao","blocks":[{"text":"Curaçao, officially the Country of Curaçao, is a constituent country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands. It is an island country located in the southern Caribbean Sea, specifically the Dutch Caribbean region, about 65 km (40 mi) north of Venezuela and 80 km (50 mi) southeast of Aruba. Curaçao includes the main island of Curaçao, and the much smaller, uninhabited island of Klein Curaçao."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Flag_of_Cura%C3%A7ao.svg/330px-Flag_of_Cura%C3%A7ao.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cura%C3%A7ao","wikidata":"Q25279"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Cura%C3%A7ao1836.png/500px-Cura%C3%A7ao1836.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Cura%C3%A7ao1836.png/1280px-Cura%C3%A7ao1836.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Cura%C3%A7ao1836.png/500px-Cura%C3%A7ao1836.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map of Curaçao in 1836","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACura%C3%A7ao1836.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Curacao%2C_1807_RCIN_735132_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Curacao%2C_1807_RCIN_735132_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Curacao%2C_1807_RCIN_735132_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Curacao%2C_1807_RCIN_735132_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Curacao%2C_1807_RCIN_735132_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Curacao%2C_1807_RCIN_735132_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Royal Navy warships under Charles Brisbane capturing Curaçao on 1 January 1807","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACuracao%2C_1807_RCIN_735132_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"http